capability open/default-application  a URL or a file opened in the application the user's system keeps for it
  heroes  os.open_url, os.open_path
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  webbrowser.open, webbrowser.open_new, webbrowser.open_new_tab, os.startfile (Windows alone)
  note    added 2026-11-17, from the community census: `open` on Darwin and `xdg-open` on every other POSIX, started by `posix_spawn` with the target as their one argument, no shell between, the signals at their defaults, and waited for; `ShellExecuteW` with the verb `open` on Windows (`hero_sys_open`). A URL is checked first: `http`, `https` or `mailto` alone, since another scheme starts whatever program registered it, no white space and no control character, a host after `//`; a path must name something, asked before the launcher runs, and one that begins with `-` is handed as `./-...` so no launcher reads it as an option. Python's webbrowser chooses among browsers and falls back to a text browser; here the system's own choice is the one, and a new window or tab is the browser's to decide. Run on this Mac on a path that is not there (`open` printed its refusal and ended 1, `failed`); a URL and a file actually opened: unrun, since a test that opens a window on the machine running it is not a test to run unattended

capability open/reveal  a file or a folder shown selected in the system's file manager, Finder, Explorer or the desktop's own
  heroes  os.reveal_path
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    added 2026-11-24 (gap G10, the ui catalogue's SY10). None of Go 1.27.4's `os`, Rust 1.100.0's `std` or Python 3.15.2's `os` shows a file in the file manager; Electron's `shell.showItemInFolder` and Java's `Desktop.browseFileDirectory` do, and the route is theirs: on Darwin `/usr/bin/open -R`, started and waited for as `open_path` starts `open`; on Linux the freedesktop.org interface `org.freedesktop.FileManager1.ShowItems` over the session's D-Bus by `dbus-send`, the item named by a `file:` URI of its folder's real path and its own name (so a link is shown as itself, every byte past RFC 3986's unreserved ones written `%XX`, a `,` among them, which `dbus-send` would read as the end of an element), and where no file manager answers it the folder opened by `xdg-open` with nothing selected, as Chromium's `ShowItemInFolder` falls back; on Windows `SHOpenFolderAndSelectItems` on the item's full path. The path is asked to name something before any launcher runs, and one that begins with `-` is handed as `./-...`. Run on the Windows box in the desktop session: Explorer opened the folder with the file focused and selected, read back through `Shell.Application`'s windows. In the Linux arm64 image, which has neither `dbus-send` nor `xdg-open`, it answered `not_found`. Unrun: a reveal on this Mac (a test that opens a window on the machine running it is not one to run unattended; `open -R` of a path that is not there ended 1 there) and on a Linux desktop with a file manager. Registering a program for a file type (the ui catalogue's SY12) is the package `ui`'s, since a type is registered by an application's bundle, its ProgID or its `.desktop` file

