capability user/lookup  a user found by its name or by its id: its id, name, primary group and home
  heroes  os.user_named, os.user_identified, os.User, os.AccountId
  go      user.Lookup, user.LookupId, user.User, user.UnknownUserError, user.UnknownUserIdError
  rust    -
  python  pwd.getpwnam, pwd.getpwuid, pwd.struct_passwd
  note    added 2026-11-23 (gap G12). Rust 1.90.0's `std` has no user database (read this session: `std::os::unix::process::CommandExt` has `uid`, `gid` and `groups`, which set a child's, see user/change). An id is an `AccountId`, a number on Darwin and Linux and a SID on Windows, so `fs.set_owner`'s number is a `match` away and Windows is never forgotten; Go's `Uid` string and Python's int are the two halves. `UnknownUserError` and `UnknownUserIdError` are `not_found`, and so is a number no user holds (run this session: Go 1.27.4 `user: unknown userid 4294967295`, Python 3.15.2 `KeyError`); a number past 32 bits is `invalid`, where Go's `LookupId("4294967296")` answers root, the number wrapped to 0. Measured on this Mac against Go's `user.LookupId` and Python's `pwd`: root (0, `/var/root`, group 0) and the user running (501, `/Users/joseph`, group 20) equal in all three; Windows against `Get-LocalUser` and `net user` on the box: the Administrator's SID ending `-500`, its primary group `-513` (`None`), home `C:\Users\Administrator`. Windows adds `DOMAIN\user` and `user@domain` to a name, and counts the service accounts `SYSTEM`, `LOCAL SERVICE` and `NETWORK SERVICE`, which its lookup calls well-known groups, as users, since they own processes; the home is the profile list's `ProfileImagePath`, absent for a user that never logged on, and the primary group the local accounts' `NetUserGetInfo` tells, absent for an account of a domain this machine cannot ask (unrun here: no domain). A name that is not UTF-8 is refused `invalid` on Windows and read as the bytes the system holds on POSIX

capability user/list  every user of the machine
  heroes  refused: a directory service may hold millions of users, so a list of them all is a call that may wait on the network and hand back a table no program wants whole; the question programs ask is one user's or a user's groups, `user_named` and `groups_of`; a program that wants `/etc/passwd`'s lines reads that file with `fs`
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  pwd.getpwall, grp.getgrall
  note    the one refusal of the lookups; the same reason holds for the groups

