  note    added 2026-11-23 (gap G12). The groups come from the system's record of groups, not from the groups this process runs with, which are `user/groups`; on POSIX `getgrouplist`, the user's primary group among them, each once and in the system's order. Measured on this Mac against `id`, Go's `GroupIds` and Python's `os.getgrouplist` for the user running: the same 16 groups in the same order (Darwin keeps at most `NGROUPS_MAX`, 16, for a user); in the Linux arm64 container for root: group 0 alone. On Windows `NetUserGetLocalGroups` with indirect membership, the Administrator's `Administrators` equal to `net user Administrator`'s local group memberships

capability user/name  the name of the user the process runs as
  heroes  os.user_name
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  getpass.getuser, os.getlogin
  note    the system's record of the effective user first (`getpwuid_r`), and `USER` only where no record names one; Python's getuser reads LOGNAME, USER and USERNAME first, which any program's parent may set to anything. On Windows `GetUserNameW`, the name without its domain. `not_found` where neither names one

capability user/change  the user and the groups the process runs as changed to a named user's
  heroes  os.change_user
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  os.setuid, os.setgid, os.initgroups
  note    added 2026-11-23 (gap G12). Written as one whole drop, `initgroups`, `setgid`, `setuid` in that order, then every id checked and `setuid(0)` asked and required to be refused where the user is not root (CERT's POS36-C), since a process whose saved id is still root can take it back: the mistake a daemon dropping root makes with a lone `setuid`. A process that is not root changes nothing and answers where it already is that user and group (`permission` otherwise). Run on Linux arm64 as root in a container: ids 0 to 65534 on all of real, effective and saved, `setuid(0)` and `seteuid(0)` refused after, the groups one (`nogroup`); and as user 1000, `permission`. Run on Darwin as an ordinary user only: its root path is unrun here, no root being had, and its calls are the same four. Windows answers `unsupported` in words: a process keeps the user it started as, and a thread takes another's rights only with that user's password (`LogonUser`), which nothing here is given. Rust's `CommandExt::uid`, `gid` and `groups` change a child's, which belongs to `process`

