capability machine/memory  the machine's memory, all of it and what is available to programs now
  heroes  os.memory, os.Memory
  go      syscall.Sysinfo, syscall.Sysinfo_t
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    added 2026-11-17 (queue 46); none of the three offers it on every system: Go's `syscall.Sysinfo` is Linux's alone and its `Freeram` leaves the kernel's cache out, Python reaches the total through `os.sysconf("SC_PHYS_PAGES")` times `SC_PAGE_SIZE` (block machine/limits; 25769803776 here, as `os.memory` and `sysctl hw.memsize` answered) and the free pages through `SC_AVPHYS_PAGES` on Linux alone (absent here); the available is each system's own word, measured: Darwin's free and inactive pages (10579836928 here, against `vm_stat`'s free, speculative and inactive pages read a moment apart), Linux's `MemAvailable` (equal to `/proc/meminfo`'s in the arm64 container, plain and sanitized, and the `sysinfo` fallback equal to MemFree and Buffers there), Windows' `ullAvailPhys` (unrun, the box was off); a container's limit is read by none

capability machine/cpu-times  the time the machine's processors have spent, and the share of it that was busy between two readings
  heroes  os.cpu_times, os.CpuTimes, os.cpu_usage
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    added 2026-11-17 (queue 58); psutil's `cpu_times` and `cpu_percent` (PyPI's 101st) the yardstick, none of the three offering it: the machine's sums in integer microseconds where psutil answers float seconds (nanoseconds summed over 256 processors pass 64 bits within a year and a half), `nice` inside `user_us`, `irq` and `softirq` inside `system_us`, `iowait` as `waiting_us` and `steal` as `stolen_us`, guests' time left in the user's where Linux counts it; `cpu_usage` takes two readings the program made, where `cpu_percent(interval)` sleeps between them itself (`os` sleeps not), and counts the user's, the system's and the stolen time busy, a span with no tick 0.0 as psutil answers. Measured 2026-11-17: Darwin's ticks are 1/100 s (`kern.clockrate`; 1593 over 2 s on 8 processors), each processor's 32 bits summed in 64; Linux's equal to `/proc/stat`'s first line times 10,000 µs in the arm64 container, plain and sanitized; Windows' `GetSystemTimes` unrun, the box off

capability machine/cpu-times-each  the time of each processor apart
  heroes  pending
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    psutil's `cpu_times(percpu=True)` and `cpu_percent(percpu=True)`; Darwin (`host_processor_info`, already read for the sum) and Linux (`/proc/stat`'s `cpuN` lines) give it, Windows only through `NtQuerySystemInformation`, whose page says it may change, or its performance counters: not written for two systems of three

capability process/usage  what a process holds and has spent, by its id: its resident and virtual memory and its processor time
  heroes  os.process_usage, os.ProcessUsage
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  resource.getrusage, resource.struct_rusage, resource.RUSAGE_SELF, resource.RUSAGE_CHILDREN
  note    added 2026-11-17 (queue 58); psutil's `Process(pid).memory_info` (rss, vms) and `cpu_times` (user, system) the yardstick; Python's `getrusage` reads only the process itself and its waited children, its `ru_maxrss` the most it ever held, not what it holds; measured 2026-11-17 against `ps -o rss=,vsz=` on Darwin (2,848 KiB and 488,785,824 KiB, equal) and `/proc/<pid>/stat` in the arm64 container (357 pages and 2,473,984 bytes, equal); Darwin's times converted by `mach_timebase_info` (125/3 here: 8,516,289 units were `getrusage`'s 0.354845 s); Darwin refuses another user's process (`permission`, pid 1 here) where Linux reads it; Windows' virtual is the bytes committed for the process (`PagefileUsage`), its resident the working set, unrun; a child's time once it ended is `process.cpu_of`'s, and a volume's space, psutil's `disk_usage`, is `fs.space` (block fs/space)

capability process/list  every process of the machine: its id, its parent, its name, its owner, its start time, and what it holds and has spent
  heroes  os.processes, os.Process, os.Owner
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    added 2026-11-23 (gap G20). None of Go 1.27.4's `os`, Rust 1.90.0's `std::process` (abort, exit, id and the child types) or Python 3.15.2's `os` lists the machine's processes (read this session: `go doc -all os`, the `std/process` index, `dir(os)`), so the yardsticks are .NET's `Process.GetProcesses`, Java's `ProcessHandle.allProcesses` and psutil's `process_iter`, none installed here; the values were asked of the system's own tools instead: `ps -axo pid,ppid,user,lstart,rss,vsz,comm` on Darwin (this Mac, 604 processes: ids, parents, users, start seconds and virtual sizes equal for every process both listed, the resident size equal for a quiet child, 896 KiB both) and `Get-CimInstance Win32_Process` on the Windows box (81 processes: parents, names, start milliseconds and working sets equal for every process both listed). Each entry carries its start time, milliseconds since 1970 and constant while the process lives, so an id and a start time together name one process, the very reason `process`'s `child/find` is refused. A process the system hides, one whose start time cannot be read, or one that ended while the list was read, is absent and never made up: on Windows the idle process (id 0) and a process the program may not open; Darwin lists every process (`sysctl` `KERN_PROC_ALL`, where `proc_pidinfo` read 464 of 622 here and answered EPERM for pid 1 and 0), so the other user's processes are in the list without usage and the owner is the kernel's. A name that is not UTF-8 is shown with U+FFFD for each byte that is none, since failing the list on one name would hide every other process, as `fs`'s read of a folder does. Linux's start time rests on `btime`, whole seconds, so it is within a second of the true time; `hidepid` mounts and Windows' protected processes are unrun here

capability network/counters  the counters of every network interface: bytes, packets, errors and dropped packets, each way
  heroes  os.network_counters, os.NetworkCounters
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    added 2026-11-17 (queue 58); psutil's `net_io_counters(pernic=True)` the yardstick, one record per interface (a sum over them is a loop of the program's); Darwin's are the interface MIB's exact 64-bit counts (`IFMIB_IFDATA`), measured equal to `netstat -ib` (31,016,318,425 bytes received on en0) where `sysctl`'s `NET_RT_IFLIST2` and `getifaddrs` gave this process 950,809,600, cut to 32 bits and rounded to KiB; its dropped sent are its send queue's, 32 bits; Linux's `/proc/net/dev`, equal in the arm64 container; Windows' `GetIfTable2`, its filter interfaces left out since they count again what is below them, unrun

capability machine/system-name  which system the program runs on: Linux, Darwin, Windows, another
  heroes  os.system
  go      -
  rust    env::consts::OS, env::consts::FAMILY
  python  sys.platform, platform.system, platform.platform, os.name
  note    a variant, `darwin`, `linux`, `windows` or `other`, where Rust and Python answer text; the platform layer's own arm answers it as it is compiled (`__APPLE__`, `__linux__`, `_WIN32`), so it never fails and reads no convention XDG_CACHE_HOME could imitate. `family` stays, the question of the path rules; whether the system keeps POSIX's rules, Rust's FAMILY and Python's os.name, is `os.system() != .windows`, and how it writes a path `path.system_rules()`, so no second function tells it (the coherence review's X04, 2026-11-20)

